{"timestamp":"2026-11-02T10:08:34.658Z","ordinal":38087,"type":"token_usage_record","payload":{"thread_id":"01a192c0-f277-7f60-8574-21ce71edb8c2","turn_id":"01a19b9f-f23a-70e0-bbf9-0adf509bb26d","session_id":"01a192c0-f277-7f60-8574-21ce71edb8c2","root_turn_id":"01a19b9f-f23a-70e0-bbf9-0adf509bb26d","response_id":"resp_0fd4138577b17921016ae86120b4dc81a38fa7eec8fa2b970f","usage":{"input_tokens":161545,"cached_input_tokens":160640,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":214,"reasoning_output_tokens":67,"total_tokens":161759},"turn_token_usage":{"input_tokens":15459814,"cached_input_tokens":15365632,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":31773,"reasoning_output_tokens":14616,"total_tokens":15491587},"thread_token_usage":{"input_tokens":733673599,"cached_input_tokens":727298432,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":1547165,"reasoning_output_tokens":791694,"total_tokens":735220764}}}
{"timestamp":"2026-11-02T10:08:39.457Z","ordinal":38088,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a192c0-f277-7f60-8574-21ce71edb8c2","turn_id":"01a19b9f-f23a-70e0-bbf9-0adf509bb26d","item":{"type":"CommandExecution","id":"exec-613d3de0-b8aa-413e-bef3-487c61d0491e","process_id":"11430","command":["/usr/bin/zsh","-lc","ls docs/v1.1.0/*1103*; sed -n '1,100p' docs/v1.1.0/WP-1103-ORACLE-ISOLATION.md; sed -n '1,115p' docs/v1.1.0/ROADMAP.md; sed -n '1,100p' docs/v1.0.0/CONCURRENCY.md"],"cwd":"file:///home/mike/nexus-wt/v112-wp1103-runner-reclaim","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"unknown","cmd":"ls docs/v1.1.0/*1103*; sed -n '1,100p' docs/v1.1.0/WP-1103-ORACLE-ISOLATION.md; sed -n '1,115p' docs/v1.1.0/ROADMAP.md; sed -n '1,100p' docs/v1.0.0/CONCURRENCY.md"}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","status":"completed","aggregated_output":"docs/v1.1.0/WP-1103-ORACLE-ISOLATION.md\n# WP-1103: Versioned Dataset Oracle Isolation\n\n## Specification\n\n**User story:** As an evaluation operator, I need versioned datasets to keep task stimulus separate from evaluator assertions, so that benchmark results cannot be inflated by exposing expected answers to the candidate.\n\n**Acceptance criteria:**\n\n- **AC1:** For a selected versioned example, the objective reaches the actual candidate prompt/provider-request boundary, while synthetic oracle-only assertion/validator sentinels are absent from the candidate prompt, request, argv, environment, stdin, and other candidate-visible input channels. Tests: `versioned dataset candidate receives stimulus without oracle sentinels`; `candidate launch envelope contains stimulus but no oracle data in argv env or stdin`.\n- **AC2:** The candidate's staged workspace and declared filesystem/mount view expose the task fixture repository but do not expose the dataset task directory or hidden validator files by path or content. Test: `versioned dataset candidate workspace excludes evaluator files`.\n- **AC3:** Dataset selection returns exactly the manifest-declared IDs for the requested `regression` or `benchmark` split and rejects unknown/empty splits or task IDs. Tests: `dataset selection returns exactly manifest-declared ids for each split`; `dataset selection refuses unknown ids and invalid splits`.\n- **AC3a:** Loading rejects changes to oracle records or task-tree content after manifest freeze. Tests: `loader rejects oracle data changed after its manifest was frozen`; `loader rejects task data changed after its manifest was frozen`.\n- **AC4:** After the candidate exits, the evaluator can still access the private oracle record and produce the expected score for the synthetic fixture. Test: `versioned dataset oracle scores after candidate exit`.\n- **AC5:** Run the exact candidate CLI against a local fake provider that captures the actual request after prompt construction; record one candidate invocation and validator/scorer result for each of the six manifest-declared benchmark task IDs, with zero skipped or missing tasks. Pin the candidate SHA, dataset version/split, and the SHA-256 recomputed from the final integrated `core-v1/manifest.json`; do not reuse the prior branch's `05ee2fa33ae5f6d6afc35df2e003c913de3f7bf0f817d2f76c709c16fb640d43` as current evidence. Test/evidence: `versioned benchmark acceptance records complete manifest coverage`. This establishes execution coverage and request isolation, not model quality.\n\n**Out of scope:** Modifying oracle semantics, held-out corpus data or custody; changing evaluator scoring; packaging the runtime; live inference against a real model/provider. AC5 uses the actual candidate CLI and prompt path with a local deterministic fake provider; the fake provider captures the request and returns a fixture response so the evaluator can score every declared benchmark task.\n\n**Dependencies:** WP-1103 versioned dataset loader and runner integration; current runner claims are held by root after the prior claim was recorded stale and broken; a test seam for AC1, AC2 and AC4. AC5 requires an integrated exact-candidate benchmark run and is separate from the unit regression suite; packaged installed CLI behavior depends on WP-1104.\n\n**Files claimed/current ownership:** The plan file and `cli/scripts/run_tests.mjs` are claimed by root on `v1/v112-wp1103-runner-reclaim` at `origin/main` 593d569b. The WP-1103 umbrella claim covers `eval/dataset.mjs`, `eval/datasets/core-v1/**` (public development tasks only), `eval/README.md`, and `docs/v1.1.0/SPEC-EVALUATION.md`. Runner claims on the same branch cover `eval/run.mjs`, `eval/runner_api.mjs`, `eval/runner_integrity.mjs`, and `eval/dataset.test.mjs`; that `eval/dataset.test.mjs` claim spans chunks 1 and 2 sequentially. The G1.1-2 evidence is claimed at `docs/reviews/evidence/v1/wp-1103-g1.1-2/acceptance.json`; write it only after the exact candidate CLI run completes and the final manifest hash is recomputed. Do not edit, index, or expose held-out payloads.\n\n## Plan\n\n1. **Reconcile the versioned dataset** — on `origin/main` 593d569b, add tests first for manifest digest/version validation, split selection, task ID rejection, and task/oracle separation; then port the public `core-v1` dataset loader, examples/oracles, task fixtures and docs. Files: `eval/dataset.mjs`, `eval/datasets/core-v1/**`, `eval/dataset.test.mjs`, `eval/README.md`, `docs/v1.1.0/SPEC-EVALUATION.md`, `cli/scripts/run_tests.mjs`. Tests: `dataset selection returns exactly manifest-declared ids for each split`; `dataset selection refuses unknown ids and invalid splits`; `loader rejects stimulus data changed after its manifest was frozen`; `loader rejects oracle data changed after its manifest was frozen`; `loader rejects task data changed after its manifest was frozen` in `eval/dataset.test.mjs`.\n2. **Wire and harden the runner** — author failing regressions first for bounded task snapshots, explicit installed executable identity, private config/key files, safe output directory, and post-exit scoring; then implement the runner integrity guard and importable versioned API and integrate it with `eval/run.mjs`. Files: `eval/run.mjs`, `eval/runner_api.mjs`, `eval/runner_integrity.mjs`, `eval/dataset.test.mjs`. Tests: `per-run versioned snapshot guard preserves the bounded dataset digest`; `importable runner API passes only evaluator config and keeps credentials private`; `versioned dataset oracle scores after candidate exit`. Keep the `eval/dataset.test.mjs` claim held across chunks 1 and 2.\n3. **Prove candidate isolation and complete coverage** — use the actual candidate CLI path with a deterministic local fake provider that captures the request after prompt construction. Separately intercept and record the exact `{command, argv, env}` launch payload handed to the sandbox wrapper, and inspect the sandbox payload/stdin at that boundary. Assert the objective appears in the expected argv and captured provider request; the sentinel is absent from serialized argv/environment/stdin; and credentials stay evaluator-side except the intended local proxy token. Keep filesystem/mount checks in the separate `versioned dataset candidate workspace excludes evaluator files` test. Score with evaluator-held oracles after candidate exit. Then run every manifest-declared benchmark ID with no skips, recording candidate SHA, dataset/split, recomputed manifest hash, request capture and validator/scorer result in the claimed `docs/reviews/evidence/v1/wp-1103-g1.1-2/acceptance.json`. Tests/evidence: `versioned dataset candidate receives stimulus without oracle sentinels`; `candidate launch envelope contains stimulus but no oracle data in argv env or stdin`; `versioned dataset candidate workspace excludes evaluator files`; `versioned dataset oracle scores after candidate exit`; `versioned benchmark acceptance records complete manifest coverage`. Requires the installed candidate and package closure from WP-1104; this is the G1.1-2 evidence run, not a unit-test substitute.\n4. **Run gates on the integrated head** — run evaluator focused tests, TypeScript build/type gate, exact project test suite, process-scope gate, and package/installed qualification on a free `SUITE`; tie every result to the final exact commit and manifest SHA.\n\n**Reference corpora:** `nexus-test` for existing CLI test conventions; `nexus-cli` for subprocess boundary and agent invocation patterns.\n\n**Retrieved references:** `nexus-test` `eval_harness.test.ts:185-211` uses a fake executable on `PATH` and captures spawned arguments/logs without a live provider; `nexus-test` `harness.test.ts:875` exercises refusal before inference. These are test-seam precedents, not proof of the Nexus runner's exact provider request or filesystem mount boundary. XERJ query: `capture spawned child process command arguments in tests without live provider`; second query: `spawnSync mock executable capture process argv and environment in test`.\n\n**Retrieval log (runner API chunk):** `2026-11-02T00:34Z` — corpus `nexus-test`, query `capture spawned child process arguments environment and stdin in tests without live provider`, hit `eval_integrity.test.ts:1220`; contract: test marker paths are passed as argv rather than interpolated into child source, used as a subprocess-test precedent. `2026-11-02T00:34Z` — corpus `nexus-cli`, definition query `agentCommand`, `MISS`; this symbol is local to `eval/run.mjs` and absent from the indexed CLI source, so the adapter was extracted from the current harness implementation and locked by a failing test before the edit.\n\n**Risk / unknown:** The current runner merges example and oracle fields into an evaluator-side task record before launching the candidate. The exact prompt/provider request and candidate filesystem/mount boundary must be observed without a live model; the fake provider/request capture must establish the actual CLI prompt path, and filesystem checks must establish access denial rather than mere omission from the staged copy. The benchmark evidence must identify the exact candidate SHA, dataset version, split, manifest SHA-256, denominator and each task invocation/result.\n\n**Coordination:** The original plan was independently approved by `review_delta` and `release_gate_audit`. The previous runner claim was older than 24 hours, its clean branch was still `c548e53e`, the host had rebooted since the claim, and no process had its worktree as cwd; `.git/nexus-claims/nexus-claims.log` records the stale-claim break at 2026-11-01T21:56Z. Old dataset and runner branches are preserved as read-only reference. Fresh claims now point to `v1/v112-wp1103-runner-reclaim` at `origin/main` 593d569b. Re-review this updated plan before coding. The previous SUITE claim was released after host reboot interrupted its run; claim SUITE before the next suite.\n# Nexus Code Post-v1.0.0 Roadmap: v1.1.0 – v1.3.0\n\n**Status:** Draft / Active Planning\n**Baseline:** Nexus Code `v1.0.0` (Release Candidate Baseline)\n**Parent Strategy:** Evaluation-Driven Development, Local-First Observability & Operator Sovereignty\n\n---\n\n## 1. Release Schedule Overview\n\n```\n2026-Q4                             2027-Q1                             2027-Q2\n───┬───────────────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────┬───►\n   │                                   │                                   │\n   ▼                                   ▼                                   ▼\nv1.0.0 (Baseline Freeze)            v1.1.0                              v1.2.0\n- Linux x86-64 Hardened             - OTel Local Tracing                - Session Replay Engine\n- Security Boundaries & Sandbox     - Versioned Eval Datasets           - Experiment Comparator\n- Ratatui TUI & Token Accounting    - Flight Recorder Redaction         - TUI Trace Inspector\n- Deterministic Packages & Provenance - Standalone `nexus eval` CLI      - Human Annotation Queue\n                                    - Deterministic Code Evaluators     - Failure Clustering\n                                    - GBNF Tool Grammar Engine          - Composio Dev Bridge (WP-1207)\n                                    - Dependabot Modernization (WP-1108)│\n                                                                        ▼\n                                                                        v1.3.0\n                                                                        - Standard OTLP Exporter\n                                                                        - Full Context Provenance (CTX-1)\n                                                                        - macOS / arm64 Builds\n                                                                        - Knowledge Freshness (CTX-2)\n                                                                        - Composio 250+ App Mesh (WP-1306)\n```\n\n---\n\n## 2. Milestone v1.1.0: Observability Foundations & Eval Harness\n\n**Target:** Initial feature release post-v1.0.0\n**Theme:** Establish the local trace standard, sanitize flight recordings, and migrate evaluation from ad-hoc scripts to versioned datasets with reference oracles.\n\n### Work Packages\n\n| ID | Title | Summary & Deliverables | Backlog / Audit Source |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **WP-1101** | **OpenTelemetry Local Tracing** | Implement `cli/src/core/trace.ts` providing hierarchical tracing conforming to OpenTelemetry GenAI semantics. Captures agent turn roots, model request spans, tool execution spans, and context compactions into local `trace.jsonl` files. | `LANGSMITH_REVIEW` §2.1 |\n| **WP-1102** | **Flight Recorder Credential Redaction** | Implement automated zero-leak credential sanitization across all recorded spans, CLI error dumps, and debug journals, validating against the test fixtures in `docs/CRED003_FLIGHTRECORDER_SANITIZATION_EVIDENCE.md`. | `REQ-DX-105`, `CRED003` |\n| **WP-1103** | **Versioned Evaluation Datasets** | Migrate `eval/tasks/` into structured datasets under `eval/datasets/`. Decouple task input specifications (`examples.jsonl`) from evaluation assertions (`oracles.jsonl`). Add versioning and dataset splits (`regression`, `benchmark`). | `LANGSMITH_REVIEW` §2.3 |\n| **WP-1104** | **Standalone `nexus eval` Command** | Implement `nexus eval` subcommand in launcher grammar, supporting `--dataset <path>`, `--runs <count>`, `--isolated-home`, and structured JSON score outputs. | `REQ-EVL-101`, `ROADMAP.md` |\n| **WP-1105** | **Deterministic Code Evaluators** | Implement modular evaluator library in `eval/evaluators/code/`: AST syntax validation, build pass/fail, typecheck exit codes, diff boundary compliance, and JSON repair rates. | `LANGSMITH_REVIEW` §4.1 |\n| **WP-1106** | **Single Step Loop Mode** | Implement `--step` flag in `nexus run` and `/step` REPL command to execute one agent turn and pause deterministically for operator confirmation. | `REQ-AGT-111` |\n| **WP-1107** | **Constrained Decoding & GBNF Tool Grammar Engine** | Implement dynamic Context-Free Grammar (GBNF) generation from registered tool schemas (`read_file`, `write_file`, `edit_file`, `run_command`). Interfaces with `llama.cpp` (`grammar`), `vLLM` (`guided_json`), and `Ollama` (`format`). Supports hybrid grammars (`<think>...</think>` + tool call) for reasoning models (DeepSeek R1). Targets near-zero JSON argument parse errors and enables reliable agentic execution on 7B/14B Q4_K_M local models. | `POSITIONING.md` §3.6, `SPEC-EVALUATION.md` §6 |\n| **WP-1108** | **Zero Technical Debt & Dependency Modernization** | Triage, validate, and integrate the 27 post-v1.0.0 Dependabot PRs (#103–#129) under an absolute zero-deprecation policy for third-party libraries. Categorized across 5 staged batches: Core Rust runtime (#105, #107, #110, #113), GitHub Actions CI workflows (#103, #104, #106, #108, #112), Cloudflare micro-workers (#109, #115–#118, #121–#123, #125–#128), TypeScript compiler (#119, #124, #129), and CLI runtime migrations (#111, #114, #120 including Commander 12→15 compatibility audit). Enforces continuous zero-technical-debt invariants. | PRs #103–#129, `ROADMAP.md` §2.1 |\n\n### v1.1.0 Release Gates\n\n- **Gate G1.1-1 (Zero-Leak Tracing):** Local trace engine produces valid OTel GenAI spans for all turn steps without leaking environment credentials or writing outside `~/.local/share/nexus/traces/`.\n- **Gate G1.1-2 (Dataset Decoupling):** Evaluator executes 100% of benchmark tasks with zero leakage of `oracles.jsonl` to agent prompt context.\n- **Gate G1.1-3 (CLI Parity):** `nexus eval` executes cleanly from packaged binary installations without developer dependencies.\n- **Gate G1.1-4 (Constrained Decoding Reliability):** GBNF-constrained execution achieves >=99.5% valid JSON tool call syntax (minimizing syntax repair retries) across supported local inference endpoints (llama.cpp, vLLM, Ollama).\n- **Gate G1.1-5 (Zero Technical Debt & Zero Deprecated Packages):** Strictly 0 deprecated packages, 0 unmaintained dependencies, 0 CodeQL alerts, and 0 compiler warnings repository-wide. All 27 Dependabot PR upgrades pass unit/PTY suites, `cargo deny check`, automated npm registry deprecation scans, package reproducibility (`cmp`), and CLI option compatibility without breaking unprivileged sandbox invariants.\n\n### 2.1 Dependabot Triage & Integration Matrix (WP-1108)\n\nFollowing the PR #8 integration merge to `main`, Dependabot opened 27 automated dependency update pull requests (PRs #103–#129). These are triaged into 5 distinct integration batches scheduled for the v1.1.0 development cycle (deferred from v1.0.0):\n\n#### Batch 1: Core Rust Engine Runtime (Low Risk / Initial v1.1.0 Release Cycle)\nPatches to Rust core dependencies that preserve API compatibility and improve stability and performance:\n- **PR #105:** `tokio` (1.53.1 → 1.53.3 in `/core`) — Async runtime maintenance patch.\n- **PR #107:** `similar` (2.7.0 → 3.2.0 in `/core`) — Text diffing library major SemVer upgrade.\n- **PR #110:** `libc` (0.2.189 → 0.2.191 in `/core`) — System call bindings patch.\n- **PR #113:** `serde_json` (1.0.151 → 1.0.154 in `/core`) — Serialization optimization patch.\n- *Verification Requirement:* `cargo test --locked --manifest-path core/Cargo.toml` under isolated HOME; bit-for-bit packaging `cmp` check.\n\n#### Batch 2: Cloudflare Experimental Micro-Workers (Low Risk / Isolated)\nMaintenance updates for the isolated Cloudflare artifacts vertical slice (retired contest contract track):\n- **PRs #125, #126, #127, #128:** `vite` (8.3.3 → 8.3.4 across root and worker packages).\n- **PRs #115, #117, #118:** `@cloudflare/vite-plugin` (2.0.0-beta.sha-52b0dc0e9 → 2.0.0-beta.v.1).\n- **PRs #109, #116, #121, #122, #123:** `cf` (1.0.0-beta.10 → 1.0.0-beta.18 across worker bundles).\n- *Verification Requirement:* `npm run build --prefix experiments/cloudflare-artifacts-vertical-slice` and typecheck pass.\n\n#### Batch 3: GitHub Actions CI Workflows (Medium Risk / CI Pipeline Validation)\nModernization of hosted automation actions in `.github/workflows/`:\n- **PR #103:** `actions/attest-build-provenance` (commit hash update for SLSA build provenance).\n- **PR #104:** `actions/checkout` (v4.4.0 → v7.0.1) — Major runner checkout upgrade.\n- **PR #106:** `actions/download-artifact` (v4.3.0 → v8.0.2) — Artifact fetcher upgrade.\n- **PR #108:** `softprops/action-gh-release` (v2.6.2 → v3.0.3) — GitHub release asset upload upgrade.\n- **PR #112:** `actions/setup-node` (v4.4.0 → v7.1.0) — Node environment provisioning upgrade.\n- *Verification Requirement:* Staged workflow validation on test branch; assert identical artifact download and release asset publishing behavior.\n\n#### Batch 4: TypeScript Compiler & Typing Toolchain (Medium Risk / Type Safety Validation)\nModernization of the TypeScript compiler toolchain:\n- **PR #119:** `typescript` (5.9.3 → 7.0.2 in `/demo`) — Demo runner compiler bump.\n- **PR #124:** `@types/node` (22.20.4 → 26.6.5 in `/cli`) — Node.js runtime typings.\n- **PR #129:** `typescript` (5.9.3 → 7.0.2 in `/cli`) — Core CLI compiler bump.\n- *Verification Requirement:* Zero TypeScript compiler errors (`npm run build --prefix cli`, `tsc --noEmit`), clean declaration emission, and zero new `any` casts.\n\n#### Batch 5: Production CLI Runtime Libraries (High Risk / Breaking API Migration)\nMajor version bumps requiring focused API compatibility audits and code changes:\n- **PR #111:** `dotenv` (16.6.1 → 18.0.8 in `/cli`) — Environment loading library update. Verify untrusted workspace `.env` rejection invariants (REQ-SEC-001) remain strictly intact.\n- **PR #114:** `commander` (12.1.0 → 15.0.0 in `/cli`) — Major CLI option parser upgrade. Requires comprehensive audit of subcommands (`nexus run`, `check`, `models`, `upgrade`), option parsing (`--strict-token-count`, `--isolated-home`), and exit code propagation.\n- **PR #120:** `ora` (8.2.0 → 9.4.1 in `/cli`) — Terminal spinner library update. Validate stdout/stderr streaming, non-interactive CI fallback, and Ratatui TUI coexistence.\n- *Verification Requirement:* Full CLI unit and PTY suite (1,511+ tests), CLI journey tests (`cli/test/system_journey.test.ts`), and doctor verification.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Milestone v1.2.0: Experiment Comparison, Replay & Annotation\n\n**Target:** Second feature release post-v1.0.0\n**Theme:** Diagnostic reproducibility, statistical comparison across agent versions, interactive TUI trace inspection, and human annotation workflows.\n\n### Work Packages\n\n| ID | Title | Summary & Deliverables | Backlog / Audit Source |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **WP-1201** | **Deterministic Session Replay** | Implement `nexus replay <trace-id>`: Re-executes tool calls and container steps from a recorded trace inside an isolated bubblewrap container to reproduce bugs without re-calling the model. | `LANGSMITH_REVIEW` §1.1 |\n| **WP-1202** | **Experiment Comparator** | Implement `eval/compare.mjs` comparing two experiment runs with Wilson score 95% confidence intervals, token cost deltas, duration deltas, and regression matrices. | `LANGSMITH_REVIEW` §2.4 |\n# Running multiple agents on this plan without collisions\n\nThese rules apply to every agent (Claude Code, Codex, Cursor, Nexus itself, or a human) that implements any part of this package at the same time as another. The repository's root `AGENTS.md` points here. The rules come from incidents recorded in `tasks/lessons.md`:\n\n- a concurrent agent overwrote newer evidence;\n- full test suites were contaminated by edits made mid-run;\n- `pkill -f` killed the agent's own shell;\n- HEAD moved during a pause.\n\nThey also follow from audit finding PER-03: the test suite writes into the real `~/.nexus`.\n\n## 1. Unit of work: one claimed PR slice per agent\n\n- The unit of concurrent work is **one PR slice** from a plan, for example `PLAN-01/PR-1a`. Every slice lists its tasks and its safe change boundary in its plan.\n- An agent works on exactly one claimed slice at a time.\n- An agent must not edit files outside its slice's boundary. The exceptions are the ledger rows and the shared files covered by §4. If the work needs a file outside the boundary, the agent stops and records a **boundary request** (§6). It does not edit the file.\n\n## 2. Claiming a slice (atomic and machine-local)\n\nClaims live in the repository's shared git directory. They are visible to every worktree, and they are never committed.\n\n```sh\nCLAIMS=\"$(git rev-parse --git-common-dir)/nexus-claims\"\nmkdir -p \"$CLAIMS\"\nSLICE=\"PLAN-01_PR-1a\"\nif mkdir \"$CLAIMS/$SLICE\" 2>/dev/null; then          # mkdir is atomic: exactly one agent wins\n  printf '{\"agent\":\"%s\",\"branch\":\"%s\",\"since\":\"%s\",\"pid\":%s}\\n' \\\n    \"$AGENT_NAME\" \"v1/plan-01-pr-1a\" \"$(date -u +%FT%TZ)\" \"$$\" > \"$CLAIMS/$SLICE/owner.json\"\nelse\n  echo \"claimed by: $(cat \"$CLAIMS/$SLICE/owner.json\")\"; exit 1\nfi\n```\n\n- **Release** the claim by deleting its directory (`rm -r \"$CLAIMS/$SLICE\"`) after the slice merges or is abandoned. Write one line about the outcome to `$CLAIMS/../nexus-claims.log`.\n- **Hot-file claims** use the same mechanism with the name `FILE_<path with / replaced by _>`, for example `FILE_cli_src_agent.ts`. See §4.\n- **Stale claims.** A claim older than 24 h whose PID is gone may be broken, but only by the owner, or by an agent that first records the broken claim in the log.\n- If the MCP Agent Mail server is available, its file reservations (`file_reservation_paths`) may be used as well. The git-common-dir claim stays authoritative, because every agent can see it without extra tooling.\n\n## 3. Isolation: worktree, branch, HOME, ports, processes\n\n| Resource | Rule |\n|---|---|\n| Source | One `git worktree` per claimed slice: `git worktree add ../nexus-wt/<slice> -b v1/<plan>-<slice> origin/main`. Never edit `~/nexus-code` itself. It is the owner's checkout, and `/usr/local/bin/nexus` runs from it. |\n| Branch | `v1/plan-NN-pr-Nx`. One branch per slice. Never commit to `main` directly. |\n| HOME and state | Until PLAN-08 PR-8a lands, every test, build and probe run sets `HOME=$(mktemp -d)` and `XDG_CONFIG_HOME=\"$HOME/.config\"`. After PR-8a, the test setup enforces this, but scripts and probes outside `npm test` must still set it. **Never** run anything that writes to the real `~/.nexus` or `~/.config/nexus`. |\n| Test runs | Full suites run on a **snapshot copy** of the worktree (`git worktree add` at the commit under test, or `git archive`), never on the tree being edited. `tsx` reads `src/` live. |\n| Builds | Each worktree builds into its own `core/target`, and `cli/dist` is per worktree. To share the Rust build cache, set `CARGO_TARGET_DIR` per worktree, never to one shared directory. |\n| Ports | Mock servers bind `127.0.0.1:0` (an ephemeral port) and publish the chosen port to a file. Fixed ports are forbidden. |\n| Processes | Kill only PIDs you started, taken from `$!` or `ps -eo pid,args \\| grep -v grep`. **Never `pkill -f` or `killall`.** Long background jobs are stopped through the agent's own task controls. |\n| Full test suites | The PTY and provider tests are timing-sensitive. Take the claim `SUITE` before running a full suite (`make test`, or `npm test` over all files). While anyone holds `SUITE`, nobody runs other suites or targeted test files on the same machine. Concurrent load caused a false failure on 2026-10-15: `cloud_providers` saw an extra provider request after a load-induced timeout. Release the claim when the run ends. |\n| Inference | There is one inference host (DEC-16). Any run that uses the gateway takes the claim `GATEWAY` first. That includes eval runs, live checks and stripped-proxy runs. One gateway run at a time, which keeps eval timings valid and prevents overload. Small cloud checks (E5) take `CLOUD` to avoid duplicate spend. |\n| Credentials | Agents never read or copy `~/.config/nexus/credentials.json`, `config.env` values, or gateway tokens. The stripped proxy reads the token file itself. |\n\n## 4. Hot files: serialise, do not merge blindly\n\nThese files are touched by several plans. An agent editing one must hold its `FILE_` claim for the **shortest** period: edit, test, push, merge, then release.\n\n| File | Plans that touch it | Rule |\n|---|---|---|\n| `cli/src/agent.ts` | 01, 02, 03, 04, 05, 11 | Claim required. Keep each change local to the line ranges the plan names. Rebase immediately before merge and re-run that slice's tests on the rebased snapshot. |\n| `cli/src/index.ts` | 01, 03, 04, 06, 08, 10, 11 | Claim required. **PLAN-06 PR-6b (unified launcher) lands first.** Other plans add commands and flags afterwards. |\n| `cli/src/tools.ts` | 01, 02, 03, 06, 11 | Claim required. **PLAN-02 PR-2b (signal plumbing) lands before PLAN-03 PR-3c (`safeFetch`)**. |\n| `cli/src/sidecar.ts` | 01, 02, 03, 05, 08 | Claim required |\n| `cli/src/doctor.ts` | 01, 03, 05, 06, 08 | Claim required. Each plan adds **new check functions** in separate blocks. Only PLAN-06 PR-6d changes existing checks. |\n| `cli/src/client.ts` | 03, 05, 08, 11 | Claim required. **PLAN-03 PR-3a (env layering) lands before any PLAN-05 slice.** |\n| `cli/src/repl.ts` | 01, 05, 08 | Claim required |\n| `cli/src/config.ts`, `security.ts`, `sandbox.ts`, `mcp.ts`, `plugins.ts` | 01, 03 | Claim required. PLAN-01 PR-1e and PLAN-03 must not run at the same time on `sandbox.ts` or `security.ts`. |\n| `cli/src/core/capabilities.ts` | 01, 02 | **PLAN-01 PR-1a (effect classes) lands first.** PLAN-02 PR-2c adds schema fields afterwards. |\n| `cli/src/tester.ts` | 01, 11 | **PLAN-01 PR-1c lands before PLAN-11 PR-11b/11c.** |\n| `cli/src/core/state.ts` | 04, 08 | **PLAN-08 PR-8a (HOME isolation) lands before everything.** |\n| `core/src/runner.rs`, `core/src/main.rs` | 01, 02, 06 | Claim required. The protocol version number is owned by PLAN-02 PR-2a. PLAN-06 PR-6c only reads it. |\n| `core/src/bin/nexus_tui/{live_state,live_view}.rs` | 01, 02 | Claim required |\n| `package.json`, `cli/package.json`, `core/Cargo.toml`, lockfiles | 01, 06, 07, 08 | **Only one agent at a time.** Version fields are written only by `scripts/sync-version.mjs`. Dependency changes are separate commits that touch only the lockfile and manifest. |\n| `harness.sh`, `install.sh`, `scripts/package-linux.sh`, `.github/workflows/*` | 05, 06, 07, 09, C1 | Claim required. Workflow edits are separate commits. |\n| `cli/dist/**` | all, while tracked | Do not commit `cli/dist` from slices. PLAN-07 PR-7d untracks it (DEC-10). Until that merges, only the merging agent rebuilds and commits dist, in a final separate commit, after rebasing. |\n| `docs/USER_MANUAL.md`, `ARCHITECTURE.md`, `CLOUD_PROVIDERS.md`, `quickstart.md` | 05, 09 and doc tasks in every plan | Edit only the sections your slice changes. Hold a short `FILE_` claim. |\n\n### Ledger updates (`docs/v1.0.0/REQUIREMENTS.md`)\n\nA slice changes only the rows for its own requirement IDs: the status, test-reference and evidence cells, in place. It never reorders, adds or removes rows, and never edits another slice's rows. New requirements discovered during work go to §6, not into the ledger. Because rows are independent lines, git merges these edits cleanly. If a conflict appears anyway, resolve it row by row, keeping both changes.\n\n## 5. Commit and merge discipline\n\n1. Before **every** commit, compare `git rev-parse HEAD` with the commit you last made. Then run `git log <yours>..HEAD` on the branch. If anything foreign appears, stop and reconcile. Never commit generated artifacts (evidence, dist, lockfiles) over a branch that moved (lessons 2026-10-13).\n2. Commits are scoped to the slice. Do not reformat or clean up files outside the boundary.\n3. Merge order follows the precedence rules in §4 and the ROADMAP milestone order. Within a milestone, the slice with fewer hot-file claims merges first.\n4. Rebase onto `origin/main` immediately before merging. Re-run the slice's targeted tests, plus `make test`, on a snapshot of the rebased commit. Merge only if both pass.\n5. Pushing, tagging, publishing and changing GitHub state (issues, milestones, releases) are **owner-approved actions** (DECISIONS X-list). Agents prepare them and do not perform them.\n6. Preserve unrelated user work: the untracked `nexus-continuity-validation/`, the stashes, and any dirty files you did not create. Never stash, reset, clean or check out over them.\n\n## 6. Coordination records\n\n| Record | Where | Purpose |\n|---|---|---|\n| Claims | `$(git rev-parse --git-common-dir)/nexus-claims/` | Who owns which slice or file right now |\n| Claim log | `$(git rev-parse --git-common-dir)/nexus-claims.log` (append-only, one line per event) | Claim, release, broken claim, boundary request |\n| Boundary requests and discovered requirements | The PR description, under the heading `Needs from other slices`. Mirrored as one line in the claim log. | So the owning slice's agent can pick it up. Never fixed silently out of scope. |\n| Slice evidence | `docs/reviews/evidence/v1/<plan>-<slice>/` (new directory per slice) | No shared evidence files, so agents cannot overwrite each other's evidence |\n\n## 7. Safe parallel lanes\n","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":2,"nanos":456484682}},"started_at_ms":1793614117000,"completed_at_ms":1793614119457}}
{"timestamp":"2026-11-02T10:08:41.555Z","ordinal":38089,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call_output","id":"ctco_01a19bbb-8a53-7731-b284-e56778f566e0","call_id":"call_484838d23572450a9e73dc30f63ea2f9","output":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 7.0 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"Warning: truncated output (original token count: 7820)\nTotal output lines: 253\n\ndocs/v1.1.0/WP-1103-ORACLE-ISOLATION.md\n# WP-1103: Versioned Dataset Oracle Isolation\n\n## Specification\n\n**User story:** As an evaluation operator, I need versioned datasets to keep task stimulus separate from evaluator assertions, so that benchmark results cannot be inflated by exposing expected answers to the candidate.\n\n**Acceptance criteria:**\n\n- **AC1:** For a selected versioned example, the objective reaches the actual candidate prompt/provider-request boundary, while synthetic oracle-only assertion/validator sentinels are absent from the candidate prompt, request, argv, environment, stdin, and other candidate-visible input channels. Tests: `versioned dataset candidate receives stimulus without oracle sentinels`; `candidate launch envelope contains stimulus but no oracle data in argv env or stdin`.\n- **AC2:** The candidate's staged workspace and declared filesystem/mount view expose the task fixture repository but do not expose the dataset task directory or hidden validator files by path or content. Test: `versioned dataset candidate workspace excludes evaluator files`.\n- **AC3:** Dataset selection returns exactly the manifest-declared IDs for the requested `regression` or `benchmark` split and rejects unknown/empty splits or task IDs. Tests: `dataset selection returns exactly manifest-declared ids for each split`; `dataset selection refuses unknown ids and invalid splits`.\n- **AC3a:** Loading rejects changes to oracle records or task-tree content after manifest freeze. Tests: `loader rejects oracle data changed after its manifest was frozen`; `loader rejects task data changed after its manifest was frozen`.\n- **AC4:** After the candidate exits, the evaluator can still access the private oracle record and produce the expected score for the synthetic fixture. Test: `versioned dataset oracle scores after candidate exit`.\n- **AC5:** Run the exact candidate CLI against a local fake provider that captures the actual request after prompt construction; record one candidate invocation and validator/scorer result for each of the six manifest-declared benchmark task IDs, with zero skipped or missing tasks. Pin the candidate SHA, dataset version/split, and the SHA-256 recomputed from the final integrated `core-v1/manifest.json`; do not reuse the prior branch's `05ee2fa33ae5f6d6afc35df2e003c913de3f7bf0f817d2f76c709c16fb640d43` as current evidence. Test/evidence: `versioned benchmark acceptance records complete manifest coverage`. This establishes execution coverage and request isolation, not model quality.\n\n**Out of scope:** Modifying oracle semantics, held-out corpus data or custody; changing evaluator scoring; packaging the runtime; live inference against a real model/provider. AC5 uses the actual candidate CLI and prompt path with a local deterministic fake provider; the fake provider captures the request and returns a fixture response so the evaluator can score every declared benchmark task.\n\n**Dependencies:** WP-1103 versioned dataset loader and runner integration; current runner claims are held by root after the prior claim was recorded stale and broken; a test seam for AC1, AC2 and AC4. AC5 requires an integrated exact-candidate benchmark run and is separate from the unit regression suite; packaged installed CLI behavior depends on WP-1104.\n\n**Files claimed/current ownership:** The plan file and `cli/scripts/run_tests.mjs` are claimed by root on `v1/v112-wp1103-runner-reclaim` at `origin/main` 593d569b. The WP-1103 umbrella claim covers `eval/dataset.mjs`, `eval/datasets/core-v1/**` (public development tasks only), `eval/README.md`, and `docs/v1.1.0/SPEC-EVALUATION.md`. Runner claims on the same branch cover `eval/run.mjs`, `eval/runner_api.mjs`, `eval/runner_integrity.mjs`, and `eval/dataset.test.mjs`; that `eval/dataset.test.mjs` claim spans chunks 1 and 2 sequentially. The G1.1-2 evidence is claimed at `docs/reviews/evidence/v1/wp-1103-g1.1-2/acceptance.json`; write it only after the exact candidate CLI run completes and the final manifest hash is recomputed. Do not edit, index, or expose held-out payloads.\n\n## Plan\n\n1. **Reconcile the versioned dataset** — on `origin/main` 593d569b, add tests first for manifest digest/version validation, split selection, task ID rejection, and task/oracle separation; then port the public `core-v1` dataset loader, examples/oracles, task fixtures and docs. Files: `eval/dataset.mjs`, `eval/datasets/core-v1/**`, `eval/dataset.test.mjs`, `eval/README.md`, `docs/v1.1.0/SPEC-EVALUATION.md`, `cli/scripts/run_tests.mjs`. Tests: `dataset selection returns exactly manifest-declared ids for each split`; `dataset selection refuses unknown ids and invalid splits`; `loader rejects stimulus data changed after its manifest was frozen`; `loader rejects oracle data changed after its manifest was frozen`; `loader rejects task data changed after its manifest was frozen` in `eval/dataset.test.mjs`.\n2. **Wire and harden the runner** — author failing regressions first for bounded task sna…5320 tokens truncated…s `FILE_` claim for the **shortest** period: edit, test, push, merge, then release.\n\n| File | Plans that touch it | Rule |\n|---|---|---|\n| `cli/src/agent.ts` | 01, 02, 03, 04, 05, 11 | Claim required. Keep each change local to the line ranges the plan names. Rebase immediately before merge and re-run that slice's tests on the rebased snapshot. |\n| `cli/src/index.ts` | 01, 03, 04, 06, 08, 10, 11 | Claim required. **PLAN-06 PR-6b (unified launcher) lands first.** Other plans add commands and flags afterwards. |\n| `cli/src/tools.ts` | 01, 02, 03, 06, 11 | Claim required. **PLAN-02 PR-2b (signal plumbing) lands before PLAN-03 PR-3c (`safeFetch`)**. |\n| `cli/src/sidecar.ts` | 01, 02, 03, 05, 08 | Claim required |\n| `cli/src/doctor.ts` | 01, 03, 05, 06, 08 | Claim required. Each plan adds **new check functions** in separate blocks. Only PLAN-06 PR-6d changes existing checks. |\n| `cli/src/client.ts` | 03, 05, 08, 11 | Claim required. **PLAN-03 PR-3a (env layering) lands before any PLAN-05 slice.** |\n| `cli/src/repl.ts` | 01, 05, 08 | Claim required |\n| `cli/src/config.ts`, `security.ts`, `sandbox.ts`, `mcp.ts`, `plugins.ts` | 01, 03 | Claim required. PLAN-01 PR-1e and PLAN-03 must not run at the same time on `sandbox.ts` or `security.ts`. |\n| `cli/src/core/capabilities.ts` | 01, 02 | **PLAN-01 PR-1a (effect classes) lands first.** PLAN-02 PR-2c adds schema fields afterwards. |\n| `cli/src/tester.ts` | 01, 11 | **PLAN-01 PR-1c lands before PLAN-11 PR-11b/11c.** |\n| `cli/src/core/state.ts` | 04, 08 | **PLAN-08 PR-8a (HOME isolation) lands before everything.** |\n| `core/src/runner.rs`, `core/src/main.rs` | 01, 02, 06 | Claim required. The protocol version number is owned by PLAN-02 PR-2a. PLAN-06 PR-6c only reads it. |\n| `core/src/bin/nexus_tui/{live_state,live_view}.rs` | 01, 02 | Claim required |\n| `package.json`, `cli/package.json`, `core/Cargo.toml`, lockfiles | 01, 06, 07, 08 | **Only one agent at a time.** Version fields are written only by `scripts/sync-version.mjs`. Dependency changes are separate commits that touch only the lockfile and manifest. |\n| `harness.sh`, `install.sh`, `scripts/package-linux.sh`, `.github/workflows/*` | 05, 06, 07, 09, C1 | Claim required. Workflow edits are separate commits. |\n| `cli/dist/**` | all, while tracked | Do not commit `cli/dist` from slices. PLAN-07 PR-7d untracks it (DEC-10). Until that merges, only the merging agent rebuilds and commits dist, in a final separate commit, after rebasing. |\n| `docs/USER_MANUAL.md`, `ARCHITECTURE.md`, `CLOUD_PROVIDERS.md`, `quickstart.md` | 05, 09 and doc tasks in every plan | Edit only the sections your slice changes. Hold a short `FILE_` claim. |\n\n### Ledger updates (`docs/v1.0.0/REQUIREMENTS.md`)\n\nA slice changes only the rows for its own requirement IDs: the status, test-reference and evidence cells, in place. It never reorders, adds or removes rows, and never edits another slice's rows. New requirements discovered during work go to §6, not into the ledger. Because rows are independent lines, git merges these edits cleanly. If a conflict appears anyway, resolve it row by row, keeping both changes.\n\n## 5. Commit and merge discipline\n\n1. Before **every** commit, compare `git rev-parse HEAD` with the commit you last made. Then run `git log <yours>..HEAD` on the branch. If anything foreign appears, stop and reconcile. Never commit generated artifacts (evidence, dist, lockfiles) over a branch that moved (lessons 2026-10-13).\n2. Commits are scoped to the slice. Do not reformat or clean up files outside the boundary.\n3. Merge order follows the precedence rules in §4 and the ROADMAP milestone order. Within a milestone, the slice with fewer hot-file claims merges first.\n4. Rebase onto `origin/main` immediately before merging. Re-run the slice's targeted tests, plus `make test`, on a snapshot of the rebased commit. Merge only if both pass.\n5. Pushing, tagging, publishing and changing GitHub state (issues, milestones, releases) are **owner-approved actions** (DECISIONS X-list). Agents prepare them and do not perform them.\n6. Preserve unrelated user work: the untracked `nexus-continuity-validation/`, the stashes, and any dirty files you did not create. Never stash, reset, clean or check out over them.\n\n## 6. Coordination records\n\n| Record | Where | Purpose |\n|---|---|---|\n| Claims | `$(git rev-parse --git-common-dir)/nexus-claims/` | Who owns which slice or file right now |\n| Claim log | `$(git rev-parse --git-common-dir)/nexus-claims.log` (append-only, one line per event) | Claim, release, broken claim, boundary request |\n| Boundary requests and discovered requirements | The PR description, under the heading `Needs from other slices`. Mirrored as one line in the claim log. | So the owning slice's agent can pick it up. Never fixed silently out of scope. |\n| Slice evidence | `docs/reviews/evidence/v1/<plan>-<slice>/` (new directory per slice) | No shared evidence files, so agents cannot overwrite each other's evidence |\n\n## 7. Safe parallel lanes\n"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a19b9f-f23a-70e0-bbf9-0adf509bb26d","create_time":1793614121.5554361}},"metadata":{"client_authored":false,"fallback_token_limit_override":12000}}
